##########################################################################
#                                                                        #
#                           SPECTRAL_ENGINE                              #
#                                                                        #
##########################################################################

Real-time spectral analysis and resynthesis engine for macOS/Linux & ARM Cortex-M/A targets, with acceleration support available for Metal & CUDA platforms.

With planned support for the following:

[] OpenGL / Vulkan
[] RISC-V
[] FreeRTOS
[] openBSD

If you are interested in a more detailed project description see spectral_engine/README.md

PRE-REQS

- CMake ≥ 3.x and a C/C++ toolchain (Clang or GCC)
- `libomp` and `libsndfile`

macOS: ```brew install libomp libsndfile```
Linux: install `libomp-dev` (or `libgomp`) and `libsndfile1-dev`

PRE-REQS (Contributors)

The following toolchains are strongly reccomended for contributors:

- Qemu ≥ 11.x (prefer latest)
- ARM-eabi-GCC toolchain ≥ 15.x (prefer latest)
- GCC ≥ 16.x (prefer latest)

You can refer to libs.txt under the `third_party` directory for all that is built in-source. 

BUILD

Run all build commands from the repository root.

Native build
```
  make configure
  make
```

CUDA desktop build (Linux + nvcc; use target `cuda` to build the desktop CUDA binary)
```
  make configure CMAKE_CONFIGURE_ARGS='-DSPECTRAL_USE_CUDA=ON'
  make cuda
```

Embedded-target builds
```
make configure CMAKE_CONFIGURE_ARGS='-DSPECTRAL_DAISY_LIBDAISY_DIR=/path/to/libDaisy -DSPECTRAL_DAISY_DAISYSP_DIR=/path/to/DaisySP'
make embedded_arm_restricted   # host build with embedded constraints
make daisy                     # Cortex-M firmware build (Daisy Seed)
```

The currently supported workflow on supported embedded devices is to build the segment binary offline & then either bake it into the firmware or upload it onto the devices' flash. E.g,
```
make configure
make desktop daisy
./build/bin/spectral_*_desktop ./resources/testing/shakespeare_he_saw_the_cat.wav <...> 4
make daisy-flash       # Flash the target device with the segment binary
```

Other useful commands
```
make CMAKE_CONFIGURE_ARGS='-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -DSPECTRAL_DEBUG_LOGGING=ON'  # Turn on debug logging

make clean && make clean-output && make CMAKE_CONFIGURE_ARGS='-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -DSPECTRAL_PRODUCTION_BUILD=OFF -DSPECTRAL_REPRO_BUILD=OFF -DSPECTRAL_DEBUG_LOGGING=ON' # A standard run using the
intrinsics of the root makefile (preferred)

make clean && make clean-output && cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -DSPECTRAL_PRODUCTION_BUILD=OFF -DSPECTRAL_REPRO_BUILD=OFF -DSPECTRAL_DEBUG_LOGGING=ON && cmake --build build/ --target desktop --parallel # A standard run using the full extrinsics of cmake, equiv. to above
```

Refer to:
```
make info
make help
```
for the full matrix/options.

USAGE

  ./build/bin/spectral_*_desktop input.wav [timbre] [stretch] [pitch] [n_fft] [hop] [thresh] [threads] [backend]

  Timbres: 0=sine 1=saw 2=square 3=tri 4=asin 5=para 6=quant 7=pwm
  Backends: 0=auto 1=cpu 2=metal 3=cuda 4=export

  Named options:
  -w <file>              Use wavetable file (.spwt/.bin/.raw/.hex)
  -pm, --proc-mask <s>   Processing chain mask (comma-separated names or integer bitmask)
  --cache                Prewarm backend and cache analyzed segments for this input
  -q, --quality <mode>   Anti-alias oscillator quality (CPU float path):
                         naive (default), polyblep, additive, oversample
  -S, --simd             CPU oscillator uses SIMD (default; ~1.8x on saw/square, <=1 ULP vs scalar)
  --scalar               Force the scalar reference oscillator (parity/debug)
  --q15                  Opt into the Q15 fixed-point compute domain (forces CPU backend):
                         packed 8-wide SIMD Q15 on saw/square/tri/para (~1.4-1.6x over
                         float-SIMD), scalar Q15 under --scalar; float is the default

STRUCTURE

  spectral_engine/    Core analysis and synthesis
  api/                Platform-specific wrappers
  examples/           Example applications & Demo's
  tools/              Useful scripts or toolings
  resources/          Location for auxiliary files
  build/bin/          Compiled binaries

